Stop the cs35l45 amps flooding the kernel log on every mute callback.

cs35l45_pcm_mute() is the ASoC .digital_mute op, which the core calls on every
PCM prepare and trigger, not once per stream. On this tablet that is 3 to 37
calls a second while audio is routed, and it logs one dev_info line per amp per
call, so two lines each time.

Measured on the running device 2026-08-17: 10,318 of these lines against 2
actual mutes, arriving fast enough that dmesg retained only the last ~190
seconds of history. That is not a cosmetic problem. It destroyed the ability to
debug anything else on this device, including the speaker fault that was being
investigated at the time, because every kernel message older than three minutes
had already been pushed out of the ring buffer.

dev_dbg keeps the message available through dynamic debug for anyone who wants
it, and costs nothing when it is off. The status-register dump below it is left
at dev_info deliberately: it only runs on an actual mute, which happened twice
in an hour, and it is genuinely useful when a fault is being diagnosed.

--- a/sound/soc/codecs/cs35l45.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/cs35l45.c
@@ -1271,7 +1271,7 @@
 	struct cs35l45_private *cs35l45 =
 				snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(dai->component);

-	dev_info(cs35l45->dev, "%s mute=%d\n", __func__, mute);
+	dev_dbg(cs35l45->dev, "%s mute=%d\n", __func__, mute);

 	if (mute)
 		cs35l45_log_status(cs35l45, mute);
